---
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "run-status-2026-11-10");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Know what this launch means</h2>
    <p>
      Choosing a ship should leave the player certain what will happen when they
      launch. Some ships and parts belong within the scored game's rules while
      others remain available for unrestricted play, potentially indefinitely.
      The Hangar's switch changes that promise for the whole run, even when the
      fitted ship happens to use only parts ready for scoring.
    </p>
    <p>
      The marker needs to say two things with very little space: this setting
      allows ships and parts outside scored-play rules, and this run has no
      score. A short technical label alone leaves the consequence unclear. A
      long explanation beside every part would crowd the chooser and hide the
      ship. The same small shared marker must make sense beside Start, beside
      the switch and on an unavailable catalog choice; a sentence can carry the
      consequence where the score normally appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rules are fixed: Off restricts launch, On makes every run unscored,
      and turning Off preserves fitted parts. This study concerns the words and
      marker that communicate those rules. It leaves ship balance, progression
      and the Hangar's overall layout outside the question. The current screens
      at <a href="/">the title</a> and
      <a href="/hangar">the Hangar</a>
      provide the live implementation surface. The setting name Playground is
      accepted; individual catalog class names and badge presentation remain
      open.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section><h2>02 / To be developed after 01</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>
